SDL 2.32.10 (Android): closing an AAudio output while the activity is paused.

aaudio_PauseDevices, run by Android_PumpEvents_Blocking just before the event loop blocks for the pause, paused
playback by taking the device's mixer lock and keeping it until aaudio_ResumeDevices. close_audio_device takes that
same lock first (the default LockDevice), so closing the output from another thread while the app was in the
background waited for the app to come back. AAudio is the driver a chosen output gets
(third_party/ss4s/modules/sdl/sdl.c). Measured on the SHIELD TV 2026-10-20 with the device smoke test's
audio-background case on AAudio: the close was still blocked after 8 s, and finished 10976 ms after it started, once
the test brought the app back. The SDL2 branch upstream is unchanged here.

Playback is now paused by a flag instead: aaudio_PauseDevices sets it and pauses the stream, aaudio_ResumeDevices
starts the stream and clears it -- on every resume, so a device opened after one that was closed while paused does
not start out silent. While it is set, aaudio_PlayDevice writes nothing and sleeps for the time its buffer would
have played, so the audio thread neither spins nor writes to a paused stream; the application's callback goes on
being called at that rate. Capture keeps the mixer lock: Eclipse does not capture on Android.

